<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Experiment Details</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>ResNet Architecture Details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the values reported in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 The impact of 𝜆 on the trainable depth ‣ 4 The impact of 𝜆 ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and the plots in Figures <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.2 The impact of 𝜆 on capacity and complexity ‣ 4 The impact of 𝜆 ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 The impact of 𝜆 on capacity and complexity ‣ 4 The impact of 𝜆 ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we train a deep residual network consisting of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> convolutional blocks. Similar to a traditional ResNet model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, each layer consists of two convolutions with <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,3)</annotation></semantics></math> kernels and ReLU activations. Our architecture differs from the standard ResNet in that we keep the number of channels fixed at 256 and we only use strides of size <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. These two changes ensure that the shape remains fixed as information flows through the network between layers.
Also, for our model to be consistent with the theory, we remove the final ReLU in each residual block. Therefore, a single version of our residual block takes the form</p>
<table id="A1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{ResNetBlock}(x)=x+\lambda F(x),\qquad\text{where }F(x)=\text{Conv}_{3\times 3}(\text{ReLU}(\text{Conv}_{3\times 3}(x)))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>ResNetBlock</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Conv</mtext><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Conv</mtext><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ResNetBlock}(x)=x+\lambda F(x),\qquad\text{where }F(x)=\text{Conv}_{3\times 3}(\text{ReLU}(\text{Conv}_{3\times 3}(x)))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Our residual tower consists of stack of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> of these Residual Blocks. For those models trained with Batch Normalization, we modify the transformation <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> so that</p>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(x)=\text{BatchNorm}(\text{Conv}_{3\times 3}(\text{ReLU}(\text{BatchNorm}(\text{Conv}_{3\times 3}(x))))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>BatchNorm</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Conv</mtext><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>BatchNorm</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Conv</mtext><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(x)=\text{BatchNorm}(\text{Conv}_{3\times 3}(\text{ReLU}(\text{BatchNorm}(\text{Conv}_{3\times 3}(x)))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This describes the residual tower <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> as discussed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 The residual expansion theorem ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. For the encoding <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> and decoding <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> networks, we use the same architecture as that of a traditional ResNet model as implemented in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> (see for instance <a href="https://github.com/google/flax/blob/main/examples/imagenet/models.py" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/google/flax/blob/main/examples/imagenet/models.py</a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Experiment details for Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 The impact of 𝜆 on the trainable depth ‣ 4 The impact of 𝜆 ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All results in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 The impact of 𝜆 on the trainable depth ‣ 4 The impact of 𝜆 ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are obtained by training the CIFAR-10 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> on the residual network described above. The only exception is for models of depth <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1000</annotation></semantics></math>, where the architecture’s width (or channel size) was set to 128 instead of 256 because of memory limitation; all other aspects remained identical.
Each model was trained with a batch size of 64 for 160,000 steps (i.e., approximately 200 epochs) using SGD with momentum 0.9 and on a sweep of learning rates ranging from <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2^{-10},2^{-9},\dots,2^{-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2^{-10},2^{-9},\dots,2^{-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We apply data augmentation in the form of data scaling, random cropping and random flips. We do not employ any learning rate schedule or weight decay during training.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each learning rate in the sweep, we train the model with 5 random seeds which are used for model initialization and data shuffling. We then measure and report the mean and standard error for the best average test accuracy achieved for a given learning rate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Experiment details for Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.2 The impact of 𝜆 on capacity and complexity ‣ 4 The impact of 𝜆 ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 The impact of 𝜆 on capacity and complexity ‣ 4 The impact of 𝜆 ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All results in Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.2 The impact of 𝜆 on capacity and complexity ‣ 4 The impact of 𝜆 ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (and Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 The impact of 𝜆 on capacity and complexity ‣ 4 The impact of 𝜆 ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) are obtained by training the CIFAR-10 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> on the residual network described in Section <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 ResNet Architecture Details ‣ Appendix A Experiment Details ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.
The number of residual blocks was set to <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each model was trained with a batch size of 64 for 100,000 steps (i.e., approximately 80 epochs) using SGD with momentum 0.9 and learning rate 0.3. We apply data augmentation in the form of data scaling, random cropping and random flips. We do not employ any learning rate schedule or weight decay during training.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\{0,n^{-2},n^{-1.5},n^{-1.2},n,n^{-0.8},n^{-0.5},n^{-0.4},n^{-0.3},1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\{0,n^{-2},n^{-1.5},n^{-1.2},n,n^{-0.8},n^{-0.5},n^{-0.4},n^{-0.3},1\}</annotation></semantics></math> in the sweep (with <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math>), we train the model with 5 random seeds which are used for model initialization and data shuffling. All the values of <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> above <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{n}</annotation></semantics></math> diverged in that setting.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Geometric Complexity</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">geometric complexity</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> is a measure of how much a model’s output changes in response to small changes in its input. Models with high geometric complexity can represent intricate, jagged functions, which are prone to overfitting, whereas models with lower complexity are constrained to learn smoother, simpler functions that often generalize better.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, the geometric complexity of a model <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> over a dataset <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the average squared Frobenius norm of its input-output Jacobian:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle f,D\rangle_{G}:=\frac{1}{|D|}\sum_{x\in D}||\nabla_{x}f(x)||_{F}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle f,D\rangle_{G}:=\frac{1}{|D|}\sum_{x\in D}||\nabla_{x}f(x)||_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The Residual Expansion Theorem allows us to approximate how the geometric complexity changes with <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, the following corollary shows that for a residual models as in Equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 The residual expansion theorem ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the model geometric complexity corresponds to the geometric complexity of the base model <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> plus a term depending on <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. The fact that this latter term can be negative (as it is an inner product in matrix space) shows the theoretical possibility that models with higher <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> can be geometrically less complex than model with smaller <math id="A2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (see Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 The impact of 𝜆 on capacity and complexity ‣ 4 The impact of 𝜆 ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, right).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem0.Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem0.Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary B.0.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem0.Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem0.Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem0.Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem0.Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The geometric complexity of a residual network as defined in Equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 The residual expansion theorem ‣ On residual network depth" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is given at first orders by:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle f,D\rangle_{G}=\langle M_{0},D\rangle_{G}+2\lambda\frac{1}{|D|}\sum_{x\in D}\text{Tr}\left(\big(W_{\eta}E_{\xi}^{\prime}(x)\big)^{T}\sum_{i=1}^{n}W_{\eta}F^{\prime}_{i}(E_{\xi}(x))E_{\xi}^{\prime}(x)\right)+\mathcal{O}(\lambda^{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle f,D\rangle_{G}=\langle M_{0},D\rangle_{G}+2\lambda\frac{1}{|D|}\sum_{x\in D}\text{Tr}\left(\big(W_{\eta}E_{\xi}^{\prime}(x)\big)^{T}\sum_{i=1}^{n}W_{\eta}F^{\prime}_{i}(E_{\xi}(x))E_{\xi}^{\prime}(x)\right)+\mathcal{O}(\lambda^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">To evaluate the geometric complexity of <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> over the dataset <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, we need to evaluate the derivative of <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. We can write it as</span></p>
<table id="A2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x}f(x)=A(x)+\lambda B(x)+\mathcal{O}(\lambda^{3})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x}f(x)=A(x)+\lambda B(x)+\mathcal{O}(\lambda^{3}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\nabla_{x}\Big(D_{\eta}\circ E_{\xi}\Big)(x)=W_{\eta}E^{\prime}_{\xi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\nabla_{x}\Big(D_{\eta}\circ E_{\xi}\Big)(x)=W_{\eta}E^{\prime}_{\xi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\nabla_{x}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}W_{\eta}F_{i}\Big(E_{\xi}(x)\Big)\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}W_{\eta}F_{i}^{\prime}\Big(E_{\xi}(x)\Big)E_{\xi}^{\prime}(x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\nabla_{x}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}W_{\eta}F_{i}\Big(E_{\xi}(x)\Big)\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}W_{\eta}F_{i}^{\prime}\Big(E_{\xi}(x)\Big)E_{\xi}^{\prime}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">Now the Frobenius norm of the model derivative can be written as</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\nabla_{x}f(x)\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\nabla_{x}f(x)\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Tr}\Big(f^{\prime}(x)^{T}f(x)\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Tr}\Big(f^{\prime}(x)^{T}f(x)\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Tr}\Big(\big(A^{T}+\lambda B^{T}+\mathcal{O}(\lambda^{2})\big)\big(A+\lambda B+\mathcal{O}(\lambda^{2})\big)\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Tr}\Big(\big(A^{T}+\lambda B^{T}+\mathcal{O}(\lambda^{2})\big)\big(A+\lambda B+\mathcal{O}(\lambda^{2})\big)\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Tr}(A^{T}A)+2\lambda\text{Tr}(A^{T}B)+\mathcal{O}(\lambda^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Tr}(A^{T}A)+2\lambda\text{Tr}(A^{T}B)+\mathcal{O}(\lambda^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\nabla_{x}\Big(D_{\eta}\circ E_{\xi}(x)\Big)\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\nabla_{x}\Big(D_{\eta}\circ E_{\xi}(x)\Big)\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+2\lambda\text{Tr}\left(\big(W_{\eta}E_{\xi}^{\prime}(x)\big)^{T}\sum_{i=1}^{n}W_{\eta}F^{\prime}_{i}(E_{\xi}(x))E_{\xi}^{\prime}(x)\right)+\mathcal{O}(\lambda^{2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+2\lambda\text{Tr}\left(\big(W_{\eta}E_{\xi}^{\prime}(x)\big)^{T}\sum_{i=1}^{n}W_{\eta}F^{\prime}_{i}(E_{\xi}(x))E_{\xi}^{\prime}(x)\right)+\mathcal{O}(\lambda^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">where we used that <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Tr}(B^{T}A)=\text{Tr}(A^{T}B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Tr}(B^{T}A)=\text{Tr}(A^{T}B)</annotation></semantics></math>. We now obtain the desired result by averaging <math id="A2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla_{x}f(x)\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla_{x}f(x)\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> over the data <math id="A2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>The geometric counterpart to the residual expansion</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide the geometric intuition that underpins the functional analysis presented in the main paper. We show how the structure of the parameter space and the corresponding loss landscape provides a complementary perspective on why deep residual networks are effective and how our proposed scaling ensures their stability.
The analysis in this section stems from the same fundamental property that enables the Residual Expansion Theorem: a residual block, <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\lambda F_{\theta_{i}})(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\lambda F_{\theta_{i}})(z)</annotation></semantics></math>, becomes an identity map when its parameters are set to zero (i.e., <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\theta_{i}=0}(z)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\theta_{i}=0}(z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>). While the main paper uses this property to derive a functional expansion, here we explore its profound implications for the geometry of the optimization problem as network depth increases.
First, let us establish our setting. For a fixed dataset <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider a loss function <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(y,f_{\theta}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(y,f_{\theta}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> that depends only on the network’s prediction. The total loss is the average over the dataset:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}}\mathcal{L}(y,f_{\theta}(x))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}}\mathcal{L}(y,f_{\theta}(x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, this means that if two networks with different parameters, <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, compute the same function (<math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(x)=g_{\eta}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(x)=g_{\eta}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>), their losses are identical (<math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathcal{L}(\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathcal{L}(\eta)</annotation></semantics></math>). We also assume the loss is zero for any parameter set that perfectly interpolates the data.
A residual network of depth <math id="A3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="A3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\omega_{n}}^{n}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\omega_{n}}^{n}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, has parameters <math id="A3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{n}=(\eta,\xi,\theta_{1},...,\theta_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{n}=(\eta,\xi,\theta_{1},...,\theta_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> in a parameter space <math id="A3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Because any residual block becomes an identity map when its parameters
<math id="A3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are zero, a network of depth <math id="A3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> contains all shallower networks of depth <math id="A3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math> within its parameter space. For instance, by setting the parameters of the final block to zero (
<math id="A3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math>), the <math id="A3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-layer network becomes functionally identical to an <math id="A3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-layer network:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{(\eta,\xi,\theta_{1},...,\theta_{n-1},0)}^{n}(x)=f_{(\eta,\xi,\theta_{1},...,\theta_{n-1})}^{n-1}(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{(\eta,\xi,\theta_{1},...,\theta_{n-1},0)}^{n}(x)=f_{(\eta,\xi,\theta_{1},...,\theta_{n-1})}^{n-1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This structural embedding has a direct consequence on the loss landscape. The loss of the deeper network on this embedded subspace is identical to the loss of the shallower network:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{n}(\eta,\xi,\theta_{1},...,\theta_{n-1},0)=\mathcal{L}_{n-1}(\eta,\xi,\theta_{1},...,\theta_{n-1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{n}(\eta,\xi,\theta_{1},...,\theta_{n-1},0)=\mathcal{L}_{n-1}(\eta,\xi,\theta_{1},...,\theta_{n-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">This provides a powerful geometric explanation for why increasing depth is an effective optimization strategy. Adding a new layer does not force the optimizer to find a solution in an entirely new landscape; instead, it expands the search space while preserving all solutions found by shallower networks. This is particularly true for the set of global minima (the zeros of the loss function), denoted <math id="A3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. If a parameter set <math id="A3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a global minimum for the <math id="A3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-layer network (i.e., <math id="A3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{n-1}\in\mathcal{Z}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{n-1}\in\mathcal{Z}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>), then the point <math id="A3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{n}=(\omega_{n-1},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{n}=(\omega_{n-1},0)</annotation></semantics></math> must also be a global minimum for the <math id="A3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-layer network. This guarantees a natural embedding of the sets of optimal solutions:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{n-1}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{Z}_{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{n-1}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{Z}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">This hierarchical structure of the loss landscape is the geometric counterpart to the Residual Expansion Theorem. The zero-order term of our expansion, <math id="A3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\eta}\circ E_{\xi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\eta}\circ E_{\xi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponds to the base shallow network whose optimal solutions are preserved and embedded within all deeper networks.
This connection also clarifies the crucial role of the <math id="A3.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math> scaling proposed in the main paper. The embedded geometry guarantees that as we add layers, “good” solutions from shallower networks continue to exist. However, the Residual Expansion Theorem shows that without proper scaling, the landscape around these solutions becomes increasingly unstable due to the combinatorial explosion of higher-order terms. The
<math id="A3.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math> scaling tames this explosion, effectively smoothing the loss landscape and ensuring that the theoretically guaranteed optimal regions remain practically accessible to the optimizer, even at extreme depths.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
